WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:06.140
A gritty, violent take on one of DC Comics'
most infamous and instantly recognizable characters,

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:09.200
Joker was all anyone could talk about in the
fall of 2019.

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:14.059
How did Joaquin Phoenix prepare for his role
as Arthur Fleck, the mentally unstable aspiring

00:00:14.059 --> 00:00:17.570
comedian who snaps and becomes a mouthpiece
for Gotham's disenfranchised?

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:22.029
Would Phoenix's portrayal of the man who called
himself the Joker be better or worse than

00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:23.120
the late Heath Ledger's?

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.380
Is there a possibility for a Joker sequel?

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:27.160
What didn't viewers get to see on screen?

00:00:29.020 --> 00:00:30.020
"Who knows…"

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:35.660
That last question is arguably the most intriguing
to chew upon, as there are a number of scenes

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:38.440
that didn't make it into the theatrical version
of Joker.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.820
But unlike what the vast majority of studios
and creatives do with their films, Warner

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:47.539
Bros. and director Todd Phillips have no intentions
to release any of Joker's deleted scenes.

00:00:47.539 --> 00:00:51.829
To put it plainly, Joker director Todd Phillips
just straight-up hates deleted scenes.

00:00:51.829 --> 00:00:55.879
He owned up to that fact in conversation with
Collider, telling the outlet that he has an

00:00:55.879 --> 00:01:00.109
aversion to releasing deleted scenes to the
public, as he feels whatever makes it to the

00:01:00.109 --> 00:01:03.600
big screen should be the only cut of a film
audiences ever see.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:07.280
When asked whether full scenes were removed
from Joker or if they were simply trimmed

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:11.081
down in the editing bay, Phillips revealed
that there are, quote, "plenty of deleted

00:01:11.081 --> 00:01:13.240
scenes [but] they're not going to be on the
DVD."

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:14.180
He continued:

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:17.759
"I don't do deleted scenes [...] Deleted scenes
are deleted for a reason.

00:01:17.759 --> 00:01:21.400
I have a thing against extended cuts and I
kind of hate deleted scenes.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.450
The movie that exists is exactly the movie
we want it to be I feel like all that adding

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:27.260
deleted scenes do is confuse it."

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:31.280
Though Phillips and Phoenix built a strong
rapport while shooting Joker, they almost

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:34.820
had a disagreement over a scene that was ultimately
cut from the film.

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:39.240
As Phoenix told Collider, there was a scene
he loved that Phillips eventually wanted gone:

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:43.490
one that takes place inside the stairwell
at Ha-Ha's, the clown-for-hire company for

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:44.490
which Arthur works.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:48.409
According to Phillips, the scene was, quote,
"a heartbreaker," and one of the last ones

00:01:48.409 --> 00:01:49.490
cut out of the film.

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:54.200
It saw Arthur and his co-worker Randall whom,
if you'll recall, Arthur eventually dispatches

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:58.130
violently with a pair of scissors talking
"on the stairwell leading up to the Ha-Ha's

00:01:58.130 --> 00:01:58.840
offices."

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:03.100
Ultimately, Phoenix agreed with Phillips'
analysis and realized the moment needed to

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.260
be tossed to make Joker the best it could
be.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:06.180
Phoenix dished:

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.480
"I'd always really liked the scene.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:10.420
And Todd told me and said, 'We're cutting
that scene out.'

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:14.180
And at first I thought, like, 'Wait a minute,
what do you mean have you cut that scene out?'

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:16.480
And then of course I saw it, and it was very
obvious.

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:17.460
It has to go.

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:19.740
That's just what's so cool about movies, right?

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:23.450
You can have a great scene, it's something
that makes sense, but the movie is the collection

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:26.280
of all of these scenes and they have to work
together to tell the story."

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:30.820
That deleted Joker scene may have been rather
inconsequential in the grand scheme of things,

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:35.160
but there was one sequence left out of the
film that would have altered audiences' understanding

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:36.970
of the narrative in a profound way.

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:41.110
In an interview with IndieWire in November
2019, Phillips shared that he deleted from

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:45.650
Joker a scene that confirmed the fate of Zazie
Beetz's character Sophie, the imagined love

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:46.740
interest of Arthur Fleck.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:48.920
"I was hoping you'd come in and rob the place."

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:52.320
"I have a gun.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:54.060
I could come by tomorrow."

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:55.720
"You're so funny, Arthur."

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:00.700
At the end of the film, Arthur is seen leaving
Sophie's apartment following the revelation

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:03.440
that he's made up in his head their entire
"relationship."

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:07.920
The camera shows Arthur walking down the hallway,
causing fans to question whether he killed

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:08.420
Sophie.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:12.020
Phillips explained that there was a later
scene that found Sophie watching Arthur's

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:16.220
infamous interview with talk show host Murray
Franklin, which would have confirmed she was

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:18.930
still alive and that Arthur certainly didn't
kill her.

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:22.850
According to IndieWire, Phillips removed that
scene because it, quote, "would have disrupted

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:27.090
the film's narrative structure," which the
director wanted to be angled completely from

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:28.090
Arthur's perspective.

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:31.670
Perhaps it's a good thing that fans won't
ever get to feast their eyes on any scrapped

00:03:31.670 --> 00:03:32.500
Joker scene.

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:36.440
The film in the way audiences know it now
a billion-dollar-earning, Oscar-nominated

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:40.810
psychological thriller that transcends the
bounds of comic book adaptations may be best

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:45.680
left unchanged by the release of what was,
for good reason, removed prior to its release.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:48.600
The less we know, the better that's the real
joke.

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:50.500
"You wanna tell it to me?"

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:56.920
"...You wouldn't get it."

